# 2027 Objectives Framework

The Cardano 2030 Strategy is structured around five strategic pillars, each divided into sections covering specific areas of ecosystem development (as well as example focus areas).

To support the 2027 Cardano budget process, committees are asked to identify and prioritise the outcomes that Cardano should achieve or make meaningful progress towards during 2027.

For each relevant 2030 Strategy section, committees should draft up to five candidate objectives (so on avg 2 per pillar section), with the aim of prioritising two to three.

  

## How to define objectives

Objectives should define what Cardano needs to achieve in 2027, not what projects should be funded or how those projects should deliver it.

So objectives should be specific enough to be actionable and high level enough to not dictate how they are met.

  

### What makes a good objective?

-   Outcome-focused: Describe the improvement or change Cardano needs, not a deliverable or activity.
-   Actionable: Be specific enough that people can develop proposals to achieve it.
-   Solution-neutral: Allow different approaches and providers to compete or collaborate.
-   Assessable: Make it possible to determine whether meaningful progress was achieved during 2027.

To avoid forcing artificial one-year targets onto strategic outcomes that may require several years of work two types of objectives can be proposed:
1.  Achievement objectives: An outcome that should be realised fully during 2027 (e.g. reducing onboarding friction)
2.  Progress objectives: A longer-term outcome toward which 2027 investment should deliver meaningful, demonstrable progress (e.g. increasing client diversity)
    

### Objective Examples:

Pillar 2 (Adoption & Utility) - Section: A.2. Experience (Business & Consumer)
-   Too broad: "Improve the Cardano user experience."
    -   Doesn't indicate which improvements matter or what progress should look like.
-   Too prescriptive: "Develop a new wallet with social login and gasless transactions."
    -   Specifies a solution instead of the desired outcome.
-   Good objective: "Reduce the complexity and friction of onboarding and transacting on Cardano for new users."
    -   Defines a clear improvement while allowing multiple approaches to achieve it.
    

  

